.TH SH 1 2026-10-22 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
sh, ash, lash \- the busybox shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sh
.RI [ options ]
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ name
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B sh
.RI [ options ]
.I file
.RI [ argument ...]
.br
.B sh
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sh
is ash, the shell of busybox.
It is a POSIX shell derived from dash, with many extensions from bash.
It runs commands read from a string, a file, or standard input.
.B ash
and
.B lash
are other names for the same program, and a shell started as
.B lash
is a login shell.
.PP
This page describes the shell as w64devkit builds it for Windows.
It lists the extensions to the POSIX language and the ways in which the
shell differs from a Unix shell; it does not teach the language.
POSIX.1-2024 specifies the language in chapter 2, "Shell Command
Language", of its Shell and Utilities volume, and the shell's invocation
on the
.B sh
page of the same volume.
The Open Group publishes the text at
.IR https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/ ,
with an archive for reading offline.
The applets that the shell runs by name are listed in
.BR busybox (1).
.SS Invocation
The options, described under
.BR OPTIONS ,
include
.B \-i
to make the shell interactive and
.B \-l
or
.B \-\-login
to make it a login shell.
.PP
With
.BR \-c ,
the shell runs the first operand,
.IR command ,
as a script.
The next operand, if any, becomes
.BR $0 ,
and the rest become the positional parameters.
Without that operand,
.B $0
is the name that the shell was run as.
.PP
Otherwise, with a
.I file
operand, the shell runs the commands in that file.
The name of the file, with backslashes changed to slashes, becomes
.BR $0 ,
and the other operands become the positional parameters.
The shell opens the file as named and does not search
.B PATH
for it.
.PP
With no operand, or with
.BR \-s ,
the shell reads commands from standard input, and every operand is a
positional parameter.
When
.B \-c
and
.B \-s
are given together, the shell runs the command and then reads standard
input.
.PP
The shell is interactive when it is given
.BR \-i ,
or when it reads standard input and both standard input and standard
output are consoles.
An interactive shell prompts, edits input lines (see
.BR "Line editing" ),
keeps a history, reads the file named by
.BR ENV ,
turns on the
.B monitor
option, and returns to the prompt after an error.
It ends at Ctrl\-D on an empty line or at the
.B exit
builtin, except while it has background jobs (see
.BR exit ).
When standard input is not a console, an interactive shell prints the
prompts but reads plain lines, with no editing.
.PP
The shell is a login shell when it is run as
.BR lash ,
or is given
.B \-l
or
.BR \-\-login .
.SS Startup files
A login shell first changes to
.BR HOME ,
or, when that is unset or empty, to the user profile, the directory
that Windows keeps for the account, such as
.IR C:/Users/name .
It skips this step when it is given
.BR \-d .
It then reads these files, skipping any that do not exist:
.IP 1. 4
.I /etc/profile
on the system drive, which is the drive that holds the Windows system
directory, or on the drive named by
.BR BB_SYSTEMROOT ;
.IP 2. 4
.I etc/profile
in the directory above the one that holds
.IR busybox.exe ,
which in the kit is the kit's own profile (a w64devkit change; see
.BR NOTES );
and
.IP 3. 4
.IR $HOME/.profile ,
when
.B HOME
is set.
.PP
An interactive shell, login or not, then reads the file named by
.BR ENV ,
when that variable is set and not empty.
The shell expands the value of
.B ENV
first, as in double quotes, so that it may refer to other variables.
A shell that is neither a login shell nor interactive reads none of
these files.
The shell reads each file itself, as the
.B .\&
builtin does.
With
.BR \-d ,
the shell changes to the directory after it has read the profiles and
before it reads the
.B ENV
file.
.PP
The kit's profile is described in
.BR w64devkit (1).
.SS Language
The shell implements the POSIX shell language.
It has these extensions from bash:
.IP \(bu 2
Functions may be defined with the
.B function
keyword.
The
.B local
builtin makes variables local to a function, and
.B local \-
restores the shell options when the function returns.
.IP \(bu 2
Quoting with
.BI $\(aq string \(aq\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Substrings,
.BI ${ name : offset : length }\fR,
and replacement of the first match of a pattern,
.BI ${ name / pattern / replacement }\fR,
or of every match with
.B //
in place of
.BR / .
.IP \(bu 2
The redirections
.BI &> file
and
.BI <<< word\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Process substitution,
.BI <( command )
and
.BI >( command )\fR.
The shell passes the command a name such as
.BI /dev/fd/ N\fR,
which only busybox programs can open.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B pipefail
option; the
.B source
and
.B let
builtins; and the options
.BR "wait \-n" ,
.BR "read \-d" ,
and
.BR "export \-n" .
.IP \(bu 2
When a command is not found and a function named
.B command_not_found_handle
exists, the shell calls that function with the command name and
arguments.
.PP
Arithmetic expansion and
.B let
use 64-bit signed integers.
They have the C operators, including
.B ?:
and the assignment operators, and also
.B **
for exponentiation.
Constants are decimal, octal with a leading
.BR 0 ,
hexadecimal with a leading
.BR 0x ,
or written as
.IB base # digits\fR.
.PP
The shell lacks these features of bash: arrays,
.BR (( ,
.BR [[ ,
.BR select ,
brace expansion,
.BI ${! name }\fR,
case-changing expansions,
.BR declare ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR pushd ,
.BR fg ,
and
.BR bg .
.PP
The special parameters
.BR $@ ,
.BR $* ,
.BR $# ,
.BR $? ,
.BR $\- ,
.BR $$ ,
.BR $! ,
and
.B $0
are as in POSIX.
.B $\-
holds the letters of the options that are on, including
.B i
in an interactive shell,
.B c
with
.BR \-c ,
and
.B s
when the shell reads commands from standard input.
.SS Shell options
Each shell option has a name.
.B set \-o
.I name
turns the option on, and
.B set +o
.I name
turns it off.
An option that also has a letter, listed under
.BR OPTIONS ,
can be turned on with
.BI "set \-" letter
and off with
.BI "set +" letter\fR,
as in
.B set \-e
and
.BR "set +e" .
.B set \-o
with no name prints the state of every option, and
.B set +o
with no name prints commands that restore that state.
.PP
These options have names but no letters:
.TP
.B vi
Edit command lines with vi key bindings.
.TP
.B pipefail
Give a pipeline the status of the last command in it that failed, or 0
if none failed.
.TP
.B noconsole
Hide or minimize the console window, as
.B noiconify
selects.
Turning the option off shows the window again.
At startup, and before every
.B set
command with arguments takes effect, the shell sets the option to
whether the window is hidden or minimized.
.TP
.B noiconify
Make
.B noconsole
hide the window instead of minimizing it.
The option is on at startup, and every
.B set
command with arguments turns it on again before it reads them (a
w64devkit change; see
.BR NOTES ).
To minimize the window, turn
.B noiconify
off in the same command that turns
.B noconsole
on, as in
.BR "set +o noiconify \-o noconsole" .
For the same reason,
.B set \-o
and
.B set +o
always list the option as on.
.TP
.B nocaseglob
Match file names in patterns without regard to case.
Without this option, patterns are case-sensitive, although Windows file
systems are not.
.TP
.B nohiddenglob
Do not let patterns match files that have the Windows hidden attribute.
For a pattern in another directory, as in
.IR dir/* ,
the shell checks the file of the same name in the current directory,
not the match itself: it drops
.I dir/name
only when
.I ./name
exists and is hidden.
.TP
.B nohidsysglob
Like
.BR nohiddenglob ,
but only for files that are both hidden and system files.
.PP
As on Unix, a name that begins with a dot is matched only by a pattern
that begins with a dot.
Tab completion also honors
.B nohiddenglob
and
.BR nohidsysglob .
.SS Command search and execution
The first word of a simple command, after alias expansion, is the
command name.
If the name contains no slash, backslash, or drive prefix, the shell
runs the first of these that has that name:
.IP 1. 4
a shell function;
.IP 2. 4
a builtin;
.IP 3. 4
an applet, unless
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
excludes it; or
.IP 4. 4
a program found by searching
.BR PATH .
.PP
Otherwise the name is the path of the file to run, and the shell does
not search
.BR PATH .
The names
.IR /bin/name ,
.IR /usr/bin/name ,
.IR /sbin/name ,
and
.I /usr/sbin/name
are special: the shell runs a file of that name on the current drive if
it exists, and otherwise the builtin or applet
.IR name .
The shell looks nowhere else, so
.B #!/bin/sh
and
.B /bin/ls
work on a drive that has no
.I /bin
directory.
.PP
The
.B command
builtin skips functions.
.B type
.I name
reports which of these the name resolves to, and calls an applet a
.BR "builtin applet" .
.PP
The shell searches the directories in
.B PATH
in order.
An empty entry, such as a leading or trailing semicolon or two
semicolons in a row, stands for the current directory.
In each directory the shell tries the name as given, and then with
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
and
.B .cmd
appended, in that order.
The shell does not use
.BR PATHEXT .
A regular file is a program when its name ends in one of those suffixes,
when it begins with
.BR #! ,
or when it is a Windows program, a PE executable that is not a DLL.
The shell remembers where it found a program until
.B PATH
changes or
.B hash \-r
runs.
.PP
How the shell runs a program depends on the kind of file:
.IP \(bu 2
The shell starts a Windows program directly.
The program receives its arguments quoted by the rules of the Microsoft
C runtime, so a program that parses its command line differently may
see different arguments.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.B .bat
or
.B .cmd
file runs under
.BR cmd.exe ,
which Windows starts for it.
The status is the batch file's exit code.
An empty batch file gives a meaningless status, usually with an error
message.
.IP \(bu 2
A file that begins with
.B #!\&
runs under the interpreter named on that line.
The shell passes the rest of the line, with surrounding blanks removed,
to the interpreter as one argument.
When the last component of the interpreter's name is an applet, that
applet runs the script, whatever the directory, so that
.B #!/bin/sh
and
.B #!/usr/bin/env
work.
Otherwise the shell runs the named interpreter, and tries the suffixes
as it does for a command name.
When the interpreter is in one of the four Unix directories above and
no such file exists, the shell runs the program of that name found
through the
.B PATH
that the shell was started with; a change to
.B PATH
in the shell does not affect this search.
Interpreters may nest four deep.
.IP \(bu 2
A file whose name ends in
.B .sh
and that has no
.B #!\&
line runs under
.BR sh .
.IP \(bu 2
Any other file is not a program, and the shell reports
.B Permission denied
with status 126, or 127 when it found the file through
.BR PATH .
.PP
The shell waits for every foreground program, GUI programs included.
To continue without waiting, end the command with
.BR & .
When Windows reports that a program needs elevation, the shell tries
again through
.B ShellExecute
with the
.B runas
verb, which shows the elevation prompt.
.PP
busybox runs an applet itself, so no file with the applet's name needs
to exist, and the shell does not consult
.BR PATH .
The small applets that busybox marks
.B NOFORK
run inside the shell process; see
.B \-\-list\-full
in
.BR busybox (1).
Any other applet runs in another busybox process.
.PP
Each pipeline element, subshell, command substitution, and background
command runs in another busybox process, which receives a copy of the
shell's state.
Windows has no
.BR fork ,
so starting these takes longer than on Unix.
For the same reason,
.B exec
does not replace the shell; see
.B exec
under
.BR "Builtin commands" .
.SS Builtin commands
These names are builtins:
.B :
.B .\&
.B [
.B alias
.B break
.B cd
.B command
.B continue
.B echo
.B eval
.B exec
.B exit
.B export
.B false
.B getopts
.B hash
.B help
.B history
.B jobs
.B kill
.B let
.B local
.B printf
.B pwd
.B read
.B readonly
.B return
.B set
.B shift
.B source
.B test
.B times
.B title
.B trap
.B true
.B type
.B ulimit
.B umask
.B unalias
.B unset
.B wait
.PP
Builtins that behave as in POSIX or bash are not described here.
The others are:
.TP
\fB.\fR \fIfile\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Read and run commands from
.I file
in the current shell.
.B source
is the same.
The shell searches for a name without a slash or backslash only in
.BR PATH ,
and it is an error if the file is not found there.
.TP
\fBcd\fR [\fB\-LP\fR] [\fIdir\fR]
Change the current directory to
.IR dir ,
and set
.B PWD
and
.BR OLDPWD .
With no
.IR dir ,
go to
.BR HOME ;
with
.BR \- ,
go to
.B OLDPWD
and print it.
.I dir
may begin with a drive, as in
.IR c:/dir ;
be relative to the current directory of a drive, as in
.IR c:dir ;
name a UNC share; or begin with a slash for the root of the current
drive.
.IP
When
.I dir
is not
.B .\&
or
.BR .. ,
and does not begin with
.BR ./ ,
.BR ../ ,
a slash, or a drive,
.B cd
first looks for it in each directory of
.BR CDPATH .
When it finds
.I dir
through an entry other than an empty one, it prints the new directory.
When no entry holds
.IR dir ,
.B cd
takes it relative to the current directory.
.IP
With
.BR \-L ,
the default, the shell works out the new directory from
.B PWD
and
.I dir
as text: it removes
.B .\&
and
.B ..\&
components and changes to the result.
It sets
.B PWD
to that path with forward slashes, an uppercase drive letter, and each
name capitalized as it is stored on disk, except in a path through a
symbolic link or junction.
With
.BR \-P ,
the shell passes
.I dir
to Windows unchanged and sets
.B PWD
to the directory that Windows reports, with forward slashes but with the
capitalization that
.I dir
used.
Windows itself removes
.B ..\&
from a path before it uses it, and does not replace a symbolic link or
junction with its target, so
.B \-L
and
.B \-P
change to the same directory and differ only in how
.B PWD
is written.
.TP
\fBpwd\fR [\fB\-LPa\fR]
Print the current directory.
.B \-a
prints the current directory of each drive instead.
.TP
\fBcommand\fR [\fB\-p\fR] \fIname\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
Run
.I name
without looking for a function.
.B \-p
searches
.B C:/Windows/System32;C:/Windows
in place of
.BR PATH .
.B \-V
describes
.I name
as
.B type
does, and
.B \-v
prints only its file or name, as
.B type \-p
does.
.TP
\fBecho\fR, \fBprintf\fR, \fBtest\fR, \fB[\fR
Run the code of the
.BR echo ,
.BR printf ,
and
.B test
applets; see
.BR echo (1),
.BR printf (1),
and
.BR test (1).
.TP
\fBexec\fR [\fB\-a\fR \fIname\fR] [\fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]]
With no
.IR command ,
apply the redirections to the shell itself, where they stay in effect.
Otherwise run
.I command
in place of the rest of the shell.
The shell then exits with the status of
.IR command ,
without running its
.B EXIT
trap.
.I command
must be an applet or a program: the shell does not look for functions
or builtins.
.IP
Windows cannot replace a running process.
An applet other than
.BR sh ,
.BR ash ,
or
.B lash
therefore runs inside the shell's process.
Any other program, the shell included, starts as a new process, and the
shell waits for it.
.IP
.B \-a
passes
.I name
to
.I command
as its zeroth argument.
For
.BR sh ,
.BR ash ,
and
.BR lash ,
this name chooses the applet that busybox runs, so
.B exec \-a lash sh
starts a login shell.
For an applet that runs inside the shell's process,
.I name
has no effect.
.IP
If
.I command
cannot be found or run, the shell prints a message, runs its
.B EXIT
trap, and exits with status 127 or 126, even when it is interactive.
.TP
\fBexit\fR [\fIstatus\fR]
Leave the shell, running the
.B EXIT
trap.
An interactive shell that still has jobs prints
.B You have background jobs.\&
and stays, however often
.B exit
is repeated, until the jobs have finished and been reported, or
.B wait
has waited for them.
At end of input, such as Ctrl\-D on an empty line, such a shell also
prints the message and stays.
So that input that has really ended cannot hold the shell forever, it
leaves at the 51st end of input in a row.
.TP
\fBhash\fR [\fB\-r\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
Remember where each
.I name
is found.
With no operands, print the remembered files; with
.BR \-r ,
forget them all.
.TP
.B help
List the builtins and the applets.
.TP
.B history
Print the history of an interactive shell, one command per line, each
with a number that starts at 0.
It takes no arguments.
.TP
\fBjobs\fR [\fB\-lp\fR] [\fIjob\fR ...]
List the jobs.
.B \-l
adds the process ID, and
.B \-p
prints only process IDs.
.TP
\fBkill\fR [\fB\-\fR\fIsignal\fR | \fB\-l\fR] \fIprocess\fR ...
Run the code of the
.B kill
applet, which also accepts the job names of
.BR "Job control" .
See
.BR kill (1).
.TP
\fBread\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
Read a line from standard input, or from the descriptor given with
.BR \-u .
Assign the fields of the line to the variables, or the whole line to
.B REPLY
if none are named.
.B \-r
leaves backslashes alone,
.B \-p
prints a prompt, but only when the input is a console,
.B \-s
turns off the echo,
.B \-n
reads at most that many characters,
.B \-d
changes the delimiter,
and
.B \-t
sets a timeout in seconds, which may have a fraction; a timeout gives
status 142.
In a console, Ctrl\-Z at the start of a line is end of input, and Ctrl\-C
interrupts the read.
.TP
\fBtimes\fR
Print the user and system times of the shell, and then those of its
children.
.TP
\fBtitle\fR [\fItext\fR]
Set the title of the console window to
.IR text ,
or with no argument print the title.
.TP
\fBtrap\fR [\fIaction\fR \fIsignal\fR ...]
See
.BR "Signals and traps" .
.TP
\fBtype\fR [\fB\-p\fR] \fIname\fR ...
Report how each
.I name
would run.
With any option, print only the file, or the name, as
.B command \-v
does.
.TP
\fBulimit\fR
Accept any arguments and do nothing; the status is 1.
.TP
\fBumask\fR [\fB\-S\fR] [\fImode\fR]
Set or print the file mode creation mask.
The shell also exports the mask as
.BR BB_UMASK ,
so that the busybox programs it starts use the same mask.
.TP
\fBunset\fR [\fB\-fv\fR] \fIname\fR ...
Remove variables, or with
.B \-f
functions.
.TP
\fBwait\fR [\fB\-n\fR] [\fIprocess\fR | \fIjob\fR ...]
Wait for the named background jobs, or for all of them when none is
named, or with
.B \-n
for the next one to finish.
The status is that of the last job named, or of the job that
.B \-n
waited for, and 0 when all jobs were waited for.
A process that is not a job gives status 127.
With no operands, Ctrl\-C ends the wait with status 130.
.SS Job control
Windows has no process groups or stopped processes, so the shell has no
job control in the Unix sense.
There is no
.B fg
or
.BR bg ,
and Ctrl\-Z does not suspend anything.
The shell provides the following:
.IP \(bu 2
A command followed by
.B &
runs in the background.
The job ignores Ctrl\-C, and its first process reads from
.IR /dev/null .
.B $!\&
holds its process ID.
.IP \(bu 2
With the
.B monitor
option, which an interactive shell turns on, the shell records the
command text of each job.
Before it reads each command at the top level, it also reports on
standard error each job that has finished, with a line such as
.B [1]+ Done
and the command.
Without
.BR monitor ,
.B jobs
shows no command text, and the shell reports nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
An interactive shell prints
.BI [ n "] " pid
when it starts a background job.
.PP
.BR jobs ,
.BR wait ,
and
.B kill
accept a job name wherever they accept a process ID.
A job name begins with
.BR % :
.TP
.BI % n
Job number
.IR n ,
the number in brackets that
.B jobs
shows and that the shell prints when the job starts.
The shell reuses a number once its job has finished and been removed.
.TP
.BR %% ", " %+ ", " %
The current job, which is the most recently started job that the shell
still tracks.
.TP
.B %\-
The previous job, which is the job started before the current one.
.TP
.BI % string
The job whose command begins with
.IR string .
.TP
.BI %? string
The job whose command contains
.IR string .
.PP
The last two forms compare
.I string
with the first command of each job's pipeline, and work only with the
.B monitor
option, since only then does the shell record the command text.
A
.I string
that matches more than one job is an error.
.SS Signals and traps
Windows has no signals.
The shell runs an
.B INT
trap when the user presses Ctrl\-C or Ctrl\-Break in its console, an
.B EXIT
trap when it exits, and an
.B ERR
trap when a command fails.
The
.B ERR
trap does not run inside functions unless
.B \-E
is set.
.PP
.B trap
accepts and lists the names of other signals, but their traps never
run.
.B trap
does not accept
.BR DEBUG .
.B trap \(aq\(aq INT
makes the shell and the programs it starts ignore Ctrl\-C; any other
action for
.B INT
turns Ctrl\-C on again.
.PP
Windows sends Ctrl\-C to every process attached to the console.
A script that does not trap it stops without running its
.B EXIT
trap.
A program that Ctrl\-C ends has status 130.
.SS Prompts
An interactive shell shows
.B PS1
before each command and
.B PS2
before each continuation line.
.B PS1
starts as
.B \ew\ \e$
followed by a space, and
.B PS2
as
.B >
followed by a space.
Before it shows a prompt, the shell performs parameter expansion,
command substitution, and arithmetic expansion on the value, and then
the line editor replaces these backslash sequences:
.TP
.B \ew
The current directory, with a leading
.B HOME
shown as
.BR ~ .
.TP
.B \eW
The last component of the current directory.
.TP
.B \eu
The user name.
.TP
.BR \eh ", " \eH
The host name, up to the first dot for
.BR \eh .
.TP
.B \e$
.B #
when the shell is elevated, which Windows reports as user 0, and
.B $
otherwise.
.TP
.BR \eA ", " \eT ", " \e@ ", " \et
The time, as HH:MM in 24-hour form.
.TP
.BR \ea ", " \eb ", " \ee ", " \ef ", " \en ", " \er ", " \ev ", " \e\e
Bell, backspace, escape, form feed, newline, carriage return, vertical
tab, and backslash.
.TP
\fB\e\fInnn\fR, \fB\ex\fIHH\fR
The character with that octal or hexadecimal code.
.TP
.BR \e[ ", " \e]
Begin and end text that takes no room on the screen, such as a color
sequence.
.PP
The editor does not support other sequences, such as
.B \e!\&
and
.BR \ej .
.SS Line editing
When standard input is a console, an interactive shell reads lines with
its own editor, for lines up to about 8190 bytes long.
These keys work:
.TP
.BR Left ", " Right ", " Ctrl\-B ", " Ctrl\-F
Move one character left or right.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-Left ", " Alt\-Left ", " Alt\-B
Move to the start of the word before the cursor.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-Right ", " Alt\-Right ", " Alt\-F
Move to the end of the word after the cursor.
.TP
.BR Home ", " Ctrl\-A
Move to the start of the line.
.TP
.BR End ", " Ctrl\-E
Move to the end of the line.
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down ", " Ctrl\-P ", " Ctrl\-N
Go to the previous or the next history line.
.TP
.BR Backspace ", " Delete
Delete the character before or under the cursor.
.TP
.B Alt\-Backspace
Delete from the cursor to the start of the word before it.
.TP
.B Alt\-D
Delete from the cursor to the end of the word after it.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-W
Delete from the cursor to the start of the blank-delimited word before
it.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-K
Delete to the end of the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-U
Delete to the start of the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-L
Clear the screen and redraw the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-R
Search the history backwards for a line that contains the text typed.
Ctrl\-R again finds an earlier line, and Backspace edits the search
text.
Any other control or special key ends the search and then acts on the
line found as usual, so Enter runs it and an arrow moves in it.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete the word before the cursor; see
.BR Completion .
.TP
.B Ctrl\-C
Abandon the line and return to the prompt.
After
.BR "trap \(aq\(aq INT" ,
Ctrl\-C does nothing.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-D
Delete the character under the cursor, or on an empty line end the
input, which leaves the shell (see
.B exit
and
.BR \-I ).
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Z
Change the backslashes in the line to forward slashes.
.PP
For the keys that move by words, and for Alt\-Backspace and Alt\-D,
spaces and punctuation separate words, so in
.I /usr/local/bin
Alt\-Backspace deletes
.IR bin .
For Ctrl\-W, only blanks separate words, so it deletes the whole path.
.PP
There is no yank key; only vi mode can put back deleted text.
The editor never beeps (a w64devkit change; see
.BR NOTES ).
.PP
With
.BR "set \-o vi" ,
the editor uses vi keys.
It starts each line in insert mode, and Esc enters command mode.
The command mode keys are:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR h ,
.BR l ,
.BR 0 ,
.BR $ ,
.BR w ,
.BR W ,
.BR b ,
.BR B ,
.BR e ,
and
.B E
to move;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR i ,
.BR I ,
.BR a ,
and
.B A
to insert;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
.BR D ,
.BR C ,
.BR r ,
.BR p ,
and
.B P
to change text, where
.B p
and
.B P
put back the text deleted by the last of
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
.BR D ,
.BR C ,
.BR d ,
.BR c ,
Alt\-D, or Alt\-Backspace;
.IP \(bu 2
.B d
and
.B c
followed by
.BR w ,
.BR W ,
.BR b ,
.BR B ,
.BR e ,
.BR E ,
.BR $ ,
or a space, or doubled for the whole line; and
.IP \(bu 2
.B k
and
.B j
for the previous and the next history line.
.SS Completion
Tab completes the word before the cursor.
In the command name position, the candidates are the builtins,
functions, aliases, applets, the programs in
.BR PATH ,
and the subdirectories of the current directory.
After
.B cd
they are directory names, and elsewhere they are file names.
A word that begins with
.B ~/
starts at
.BR HOME .
File names match without regard to case, but the names of builtins,
functions, aliases, and applets match only in the same case.
The shell completes a single match, quotes any special characters in
it, and adds a space, or a slash for a directory.
With several matches, the first Tab completes their common prefix and
the second lists them.
Tab in the command name position with nothing typed does nothing, since
listing every program in
.B PATH
takes seconds on Windows (a w64devkit change; see
.BR NOTES ).
.SS History
An interactive shell with a console keeps the lines it reads, except
empty lines and repeats of the previous line.
It keeps them in memory and in the history file.
The shell appends each line to the file as soon as it is entered, so
several shells share the file.
When the file holds more than four times as many lines as the shell
keeps, the shell rewrites it with the newest ones.
The shell reads the file at the first prompt.
.PP
The shell keeps 1024 lines, or the number in
.BR HISTSIZE ,
up to 16384 (w64devkit's limits; busybox-w32 normally keeps 384, up to
1023).
.B HISTFILE
names the file, which by default is
.I .ash_history
in the directory in
.BR HOME .
There is no file, and no saving, when
.B HISTFILE
is empty, or when it and
.B HOME
are both unset.
The shell reads both variables once, when it finishes the startup
files, so set them in a profile or in the
.B ENV
file.
Up, Down, Ctrl\-P, Ctrl\-N, and Ctrl\-R browse the history, and
.B history
lists it.
There is no history expansion, and no
.BR fc .
.SH OPTIONS
These options are given when
.B sh
is started, before its operands.
Most of them can also be changed later with
.BR set ;
see
.BR "Shell options" .
An argument that begins with
.B \-
turns the options in it on, and one that begins with
.B +
turns them off.
Several letters may share an argument, as in
.BR "sh \-ex build.sh" .
.B \-o
and
.B +o
take an option name from the next argument, and
.B \-d
and
.B \-t
take their value from the next argument.
The options end at the first argument that begins with neither
.B \-
nor
.BR + ,
or at
.B \-
or
.BR \-\- ,
which the shell discards.
.PP
Only the command line accepts
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-N ,
.BR \-t ,
and
.BR \-\-login ;
.B set
rejects them.
For
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-s ,
the
.B +
form means the same as the
.B \-
form.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " "\-o allexport"
Export every variable when it is assigned.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " "\-o notify"
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " "\-o noclobber"
Make a redirection with
.B >
fail if the file exists.
.B >|
overrides this.
.TP
.B \-c
Run the first operand as a script; see
.BR Invocation .
.TP
.BI "\-d " dir
Change to
.I dir
after reading the profiles and before reading the
.B ENV
file, and in a login shell do not change to
.BR HOME .
.TP
.BR \-E ", " "\-o errtrace"
Run the
.B ERR
trap for failures inside functions too.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " "\-o errexit"
Exit when a command fails, except in a condition of
.BR if ,
.BR while ,
or
.BR until ,
before the last
.B &&
or
.B ||
of a list, or after
.BR ! .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " "\-o noglob"
Do not expand file name patterns.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " "\-o ignoreeof"
Make an interactive shell refuse to leave at end of input, and print a
reminder to use
.B exit
instead.
The shell leaves anyway at the 51st end of input in a row.
.TP
.B \-i
Make the shell interactive.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Make the shell a login shell.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " "\-o monitor"
Track jobs as described in
.BR "Job control" .
.TP
.B \-N
Before the shell exits, print
.B Press any key to exit...\&
and wait for a key.
.BR su (1)
passes
.B \-d
and
.B \-t
to the elevated shell that it starts in a new window, and passes
.B \-N
when it is given
.BR \-N .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " "\-o noexec"
Read and check commands, but do not run them.
.TP
.BI "\-o " name
Turn on the named option, or with
.B +o
turn it off.
The names are listed in this section and under
.BR "Shell options" .
An unknown name on the command line makes the shell exit at once with
status 0.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " "\-o stdin"
Read commands from standard input; see
.BR Invocation .
Given to
.BR set ,
the option has no effect.
.TP
.BI "\-t " title
Set the title of the console window at startup.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " "\-o nounset"
Treat the expansion of an unset variable as an error.
A script exits with status 2 when this happens.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " "\-o verbose"
Write each line of input to standard error as it is read.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " "\-o winxp"
On the command line, keep the backslashes in the variables imported
from the environment, instead of changing them to slashes.
When turned on later, change the slashes in those variables to
backslashes, and when turned off, change them back.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " "\-o xtrace"
Write each command to standard error before it runs, after the
expanded value of
.BR PS4 .
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
may name another descriptor.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The shell uses the variables below, and an entry says so when the shell
also sets the variable.
The shell passes the exported variables to the commands it runs.
.PP
A shell starts from a native Windows environment when its environment
did not come from another busybox shell, as when Explorer,
.BR cmd.exe ,
PowerShell, or
.B w64devkit.exe
starts it.
Several entries below depend on this.
The shell recognizes this by the spelling of three variable names:
Windows writes
.BR Path ,
.BR ComSpec ,
and
.B ProgramData
in mixed case, while a busybox shell passes every variable on with its
name in capitals.
When any of the three names contains a lowercase letter, the shell
imports the environment as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
Variable names change to upper case.
.IP \(bu 2
A name that is not a shell identifier, such as
.BR "ProgramFiles(x86)" ,
is imported under a name with underscores, and the original stays in
the environment of the commands the shell runs.
.IP \(bu 2
Backslashes in the values change to slashes, except in
.B COMSPEC
and
.BR SYSTEMROOT ,
and the commands the shell runs see the slashes;
.B \-X
prevents this.
.B "export \-n"
on an imported variable restores the value it had in the environment.
.IP \(bu 2
The shell sets and exports
.BR HOME ,
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR SHELL ,
and
.B USER
as described in their entries.
.PP
The variables are:
.TP
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
The number of the descriptor that
.B \-x
writes to, instead of standard error.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
The applets that the shell runs from
.B PATH
instead of by name.
The value is described in
.BR busybox (1).
Setting the variable makes the shell forget the programs it remembers.
.TP
.B BB_SYSTEMROOT
The drive, such as
.BR D: ,
to use as the system drive when a login shell looks for
.IR /etc/profile .
.TP
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
How busybox uses the console; see
.BR busybox (1).
An interactive shell applies the mode again before each prompt.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The file mode creation mask.
The
.B umask
builtin sets and exports it, and every busybox program, the shell
included, applies it when it starts; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR COLUMNS ,
and
.B CONEMUPID
behave as in
.BR busybox (1).
The shell puts
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS ,
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_SYSTEMROOT ,
and
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
in its own environment as soon as they are exported, and removes them
when they are unset or no longer exported, so that they affect the
shell itself as well as the commands it runs.
.TP
.B CDPATH
A list of directories, separated by semicolons, in which
.B cd
looks for a directory; see
.B cd
under
.BR "Builtin commands" .
An empty entry, or
.BR .\& ,
stands for the current directory.
The shell changes colons in the value as described under
.BR PATH .
Tab completion does not use
.BR CDPATH .
.TP
.B ENV
A file for an interactive shell to read; see
.BR "Startup files" .
.TP
.BR EPOCHREALTIME ", " EPOCHSECONDS
The time since the Unix epoch, in seconds:
.B EPOCHREALTIME
with six decimal places, and
.B EPOCHSECONDS
as an integer.
Each reference reads the clock.
Like
.BR RANDOM ,
these variables do not appear in the output of
.B set
until they are first referenced.
They are in the environment only when exported, and then hold the value
from the time of export.
Assigning to them has no effect.
.TP
.B FUNCNAME
The name of the running function.
.TP
.BR HISTFILE ", " HISTSIZE
The history file and the number of lines of history; see
.BR History .
An interactive shell sets
.B HISTFILE
when it is unset and
.B HOME
is set.
.B HISTFILESIZE
is ignored.
In bash it limits the number of lines in the history file; this shell
limits the file through
.B HISTSIZE
instead.
.TP
.B HOME
The directory that
.B cd
goes to without an argument, that
.B ~
stands for, and that a login shell changes to.
A shell that starts from a native Windows environment sets and exports
.B HOME
when it is unset, and always when the shell is elevated.
It sets it to the user profile, the directory that Windows keeps for the
account, such as
.IR C:/Users/name .
The shell asks Windows for this directory rather than reading
.BR USERPROFILE ,
but the two normally agree.
.TP
.B HOSTNAME
The name of the computer.
The shell sets it when it is unset.
.TP
.B IFS
The characters that separate fields.
The default is a space, a tab, and a newline.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set decides how the editor reads a line and
how
.BI ${# name }
counts characters.
If it is
.BR C ,
text is single bytes.
Otherwise text is UTF-8 when the ANSI code page is UTF-8.
.TP
.B LINENO
The line number of the command that is running.
.TP
.B MANPATH
The directories in which
.BR man (1)
looks for pages.
The shell does not use it, but changes its colons as described under
.BR PATH .
.TP
.BR OLDPWD ", " PWD
The previous and the current directory.
The shell sets them.
At startup the shell uses an inherited
.B PWD
only if it begins with a slash and names the current directory, so in
practice the shell takes
.B PWD
from Windows.
.TP
.BR OPTARG ", " OPTIND ", " OPTERR
Used by
.BR getopts .
Setting
.B OPTERR
to 0 turns off its messages.
.TP
.B PATH
The directories searched for commands; see
.BR "Command search and execution" .
Entries are separated by semicolons, as on Windows, not by colons as on
Unix.
So that Unix-style values still work, whenever
.BR PATH ,
.BR CDPATH ,
or
.B MANPATH
is assigned, including at startup, the shell changes each colon to a
semicolon, except a colon after a single letter at the start of an
entry, which marks a drive.
For example,
.B PATH=/a:/b:c:/d
becomes
.BR "/a;/b;c:/d" .
A one-letter directory name followed by a colon is therefore taken as a
drive, so write
.BR a;b ,
not
.BR a:b .
The value keeps the semicolons, and the commands the shell runs see
them.
When the environment has no
.BR PATH ,
it starts as
.BR C:/Windows/System32;C:/Windows .
After
.BR "unset PATH" ,
the search of
.B PATH
looks only in the current directory.
.TP
.B PPID
The process ID of the parent.
The shell sets it.
.TP
.BR PS1 ", " PS2 ", " PS4
.B PS1
is the prompt before each command, and
.B PS2
the prompt before each continuation line; see
.BR Prompts .
.B PS4
is written before each command that
.B \-x
traces.
The shell expands it as it does
.BR PS1 ,
but the backslash sequences of
.B Prompts
do not apply.
The defaults are
.B \ew\ \e$
for
.BR PS1 ,
.B >
for
.BR PS2 ,
and
.B +
for
.BR PS4 ,
each followed by a space.
.TP
.B RANDOM
A pseudo-random number, new at each reference.
Assigning a number seeds the generator, and unsetting
.B RANDOM
ends its special behavior.
.TP
.B SHELL
A shell that starts from a native Windows environment sets and exports
it as
.B /bin/sh
when it is unset, and always when the shell is elevated.
That name means something only to busybox programs.
.TP
.B SHLVL
The nesting level of shells.
Each shell adds 1 and exports it.
.TP
.B TERM_PROGRAM
The terminal program.
mintty, the terminal of Cygwin, MSYS2, and Git for Windows, sets it to
.B mintty
and sets
.B HOME
to its own Unix-style home directory.
When a shell that starts from a native Windows environment sees
.BR TERM_PROGRAM=mintty ,
it ignores the inherited
.B HOME
and sets
.B HOME
to the user profile, so that
.B ~
and
.I $HOME/.profile
are the same as in a console.
.TP
.BR USER ", " LOGNAME
The user name.
A shell that starts from a native Windows environment sets and exports
these, and
.B HOME
and
.BR SHELL ,
when they are unset, and always when it is elevated, in which case the
user name is
.BR root .
.TP
.BR _ " (underscore)"
In an interactive shell, the last word, after expansion, of the most
recent command that had words and did not run inside a function.
For a command with no arguments, this is the command name.
The shell does not export it.
A non-interactive shell does not set it, so it keeps any value from the
environment.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The status of the shell is that of the last command it ran, or the
argument of
.BR exit .
The statuses of commands that the shell runs are:
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure; also the status of
.BR ulimit .
.TP
.B 2
A syntax error, an unreadable script, an unknown option letter, a
missing argument to
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
or
.BR \-t ,
or another error that the shell reports, such as a failed
.BR cd .
.TP
.B 126
The command was found but could not be run.
.TP
.B 127
The command was not found.
.TP
.BI 128+ n
The command was killed by signal
.IR n .
For a Windows program, 139 means the access violation
.B 0xC0000005
and 130 means Ctrl\-C.
For a command that it waits for, the shell prints the name of any
signal other than
.B INT
or
.BR PIPE .
.TP
.B 255
A Windows exit code above 255 whose low byte is 0, such as 256.
.PP
The shell reduces any other exit code above 255 to its low byte, and
prints a message with the code in hexadecimal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a script, tracing each command:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sh \-x build.sh
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give a command string its own name and arguments:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sh \-c \(aqecho "$0 has $# arguments"\(aq prog a b
prog has 2 arguments
.EE
.RE
.PP
Add a directory to
.BR PATH ;
the shell changes the colons to semicolons, except the one after the
drive letter:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ PATH=c:/tools:$PATH
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the program named
.B sort
in
.BR PATH ,
instead of the applet:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS=sort
$ export BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a batch file with an argument, and show its exit status:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ./build.bat release; echo $?
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove a temporary file when the shell exits:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tmp=$(mktemp)
$ trap \(aqrm \-f "$tmp"\(aq EXIT
.EE
.RE
.PP
Start a job and wait for it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ make \-C src &
$ wait $!
.EE
.RE
.PP
A fragment of
.I $HOME/.profile
that keeps a long history, uses vi keys, and shows the current directory
in green in the prompt:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
HISTSIZE=5000
set \-o vi
PS1=\(aq\e[\ee[32m\e]\ew\e[\ee[0m\e] \e$ \(aq
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Paths may use either slash, but a backslash quotes the next character
in the shell, so write forward slashes, or quote the backslashes.
Drive letters, drive-relative names such as
.IR c:dir ,
and UNC names such as
.I //host/share
work.
The shell prints paths with forward slashes.
The file system ignores the case of file names, but patterns match
them case-sensitively unless
.B nocaseglob
is set, and the names of applets, builtins, and variables are
case-sensitive.
Device names and the other path rules of busybox are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
The shell removes a CR before a LF from scripts, from command strings,
and from unquoted results of expansions.
It removes a CR LF at the end of the output of a command substitution
as it removes a LF.
It does not skip a UTF-8 byte order mark at the start of a script, so
the mark becomes part of the first word.
.PP
Before each prompt the shell sets the console's terminal mode again,
undoing changes that a program made; see
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
in
.BR busybox (1).
When a program other than busybox, such as
.BR cmd.exe ,
starts the shell, the C runtime expands wildcards in the shell's
arguments before the shell sees them; see
.B BB_GLOBBING
in
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
A command line that is too long for Windows makes a command fail with
.BR "Argument list too long" .
For libtool, a command that starts with the arguments
.B cmd //c echo
runs with
.B /c
in place of
.BR //c .
.PP
The shell does not use file associations: a file that is not a program
cannot be run by naming it.
.PP
w64devkit changes the shell of busybox-w32 as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
A login shell reads the kit's profile from
.I etc
in the directory above the one that holds
.IR busybox.exe ,
rather than from
.I etc
in that directory.
.IP \(bu 2
The line editor never beeps.
.IP \(bu 2
Tab in the command name position with nothing typed does nothing,
instead of listing every program in
.BR PATH .
.IP \(bu 2
The history holds 1024 lines by default and 16384 at most, rather than
384 and 1023.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B noiconify
option starts on, and every
.B set
command with arguments turns it on again, so
.B noconsole
hides the console window instead of minimizing it.
.IP \(bu 2
The shell has no
.B [[
command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR echo (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR man (1),
.BR printf (1),
.BR su (1),
.BR test (1),
.BR w64devkit (1)
